th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/mixed-strategy.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/mixed-strategy.html","questions":[{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_mixed_strategy_86db3c42_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In a normal-form game in which player $i$ has a finite set of pure strategies $S_i$, how is a pure strategy $s_i\\in S_i$ related to the set $\\Delta S_i$ of mixed strategies?","options":["It lies outside $\\Delta S_i$, since a mixed strategy has to randomise.","It is the mixed strategy giving equal probability to all of $S_i$.","It is the mixed strategy putting probability $1$ on $s_i$ and $0$ elsewhere.","It is the mixed strategy whose support is the whole set $S_i$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a5db081edc1417c39439c367","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_mixed_strategy_86db3c42_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"In Matching Pennies player 1 gets $1$ if the coins match and $-1$ otherwise; player 1 plays $H$ with probability $p$ and player 2 plays $H$ with probability $q$, independently. What is player 1's expected payoff when $p=0.8$ and $q=0.25$?","options":["$0.3$","$0.35$","$-0.3$","$0.2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6740d6613db2cd3f85feea64","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_mixed_strategy_86db3c42_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For pure-strategy dominance only the ranking of payoffs matters. Which transformation of player 1's payoffs always preserves player 1's ranking of mixed strategies against every $\\sigma_2$?","options":["$w_1=f(v_1)$ for any strictly increasing $f$","$w_1=\\alpha v_1+\\beta$ with $\\alpha>0$","$w_1=\\alpha v_1+\\beta$ with any $\\alpha\\ne0$","$w_1=v_1^2$, which keeps all payoffs nonnegative"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a61e440baa8cdeca021689be","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_mixed_strategy_86db3c42_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Against fixed opponents, mixing never gives more than one's best pure strategy. In Matching Pennies (player 1 wins $1$ on a match, loses $1$ otherwise), why might player 1 still want to randomise?","options":["Randomising raises player 1's expected payoff above that of her best pure strategy.","Randomising makes the outcome certain, since the coins then match half of the time.","Randomising is required because pure strategies are unavailable in Matching Pennies.","Randomising changes what player 2 faces: a predictable choice could be exploited by player 2."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d4929f383b4934b64c84c13f","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"}]}}